SNAPSHOT TESTS
==============

Run the compiler on a project and keep its output as well as some intermediate state in files,
so that a change to the compiler shows up as a diff.
Think of them as a _contrast agent_ that alerts us to changes.


DESCRIPTION
-----------

A project's snapshots live beside it, under its urt directory: next to
`hello.urp` is `hello.urt`, and the snapshots are `hello.urt/snapshot`.  Each
case there is a run of the compiler, with the flags it was given and what it
wrote:

  hello.ur
  hello.urp
  hello.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- 1/                      a case; named, by a number unless told otherwise
          |-- args                extra flags, if any; the project is implied
          `-- expected/
              |-- exitcode        the compiler's exit status
              |-- stdout.txt      what the compiler wrote to stdout
              |-- stderr.txt      ... and to stderr
              |-- settings.txt    -saveSettings   the parsed and merged job
              |-- parsetree.ur    -saveParsetree  the program as parsed
              |-- typecheck.urs   -saveTypecheck  the signatures inferred
              |-- schema.sql      -sql            the database schema
              |-- client.js       -saveJs         the client-side script
              |-- endpoints.json  -endpoints      the URLs the app serves
              `-- server.c        -saveC          the generated C

One project can have several cases (the same program under three database
backends, say), each with its own args and the artifacts it tracks:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      |-- mysql/
      |   |-- args                -dbms mysql
      |   `-- expected/
      |       `-- schema.sql
      |-- postgres/
      |   `-- ...
      `-- sqlite/
          `-- ...

A case can go on from the compiler to the application it compiles: under
request-response/ are runs, each the application started afresh and sent
requests, one after the other in the order of their names:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- sqlite/
          |-- args                        -dbms sqlite
          |-- expected/
          |-- app.exe                     the application as last built; not for git
          `-- request-response/
              `-- checkout/               a run; named like a case
                  |-- args                extra flags for the application, if any
                  |-- env                 its environment, NAME=value a line, if any
                  |-- secret              the key it signs with: 16 bytes or more
                  |-- db.sql              the database it starts from, if it uses one
                  |-- interaction/
                  |   |-- 01-add.request  what to send
                  |   |-- 01-add.response what came back, as `curl -i` prints it
                  |   |-- 01-add.dml.sql  what that changed in the database, if anything
                  |   |-- 01-add.stdout   what the server printed meanwhile, if anything
                  |   |-- 01-add.stderr
                  |   |-- 02-pay.request
                  |   |-- 02-pay.response
                  |   |-- 03-mail.wait    time to let pass, or a line to wait for
                  |   |-- 03-mail.stdout  what the server printed meanwhile, as for a request
                  |   |-- 04-ship.request
                  |   `-- 04-ship.error   why the request could not be sent, while it cannot
                  `-- out/
                      |-- startup.dml.sql what the application did before the first
                      |-- startup.stdout  request (task initialize), if anything
                      |-- startup.stderr
                      |-- shutdown.stdout what it printed after the last, if anything
                      |-- shutdown.stderr
                      `-- shutdown.db.sql the database it left behind

A request is a method, a path, and then fields (-d), cookies (-b) and headers
(-H) in curl's spelling, quoted as for a shell; nothing else is taken:

  POST /Shop/add
      -d 'name=kettle'
      -d 'price=12'
      -b 'visitor=somebody'
      -H 'Accept: text/html'

It is sent as it stands, so it says all of it: a path has what it cannot carry
(a blank, a #, a control character) percent-encoded, a header or cookie holds
no line break, and nothing is read from a file (-d @file, -b file, -H @file).

What the server prints for a request goes beside its response, and only if
there is any: its own lines about connections and requests are dropped, the
program's debug output and the runtime's errors are kept.  A request that
cannot be sent at all (the server died, or hangs), or a wait that cannot be
done (below), stops the run: NAME.error says why, the records of the steps
after it go, and the run fails until it gets past the step again.

Runs share nothing.  Each has its own database -- SQLite, so the case's args
need -dbms sqlite -- loaded from db.sql, which holds the schema and any rows
to start with; within a run, the requests build on each other.  So that the
same requests get the same answers, the application's random numbers and its
time zone, UTC, are pinned, and so is its clock: the server's time is what urt
says it is, and nothing happens on the wall clock.  The first request is
served at 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, each further one a second after the one
before, and the database agrees; what the application does by itself -- a
task periodic, a client's expiry -- happens when the clock gets to it, at its
time, and goes with the step during which it does: tasks due when a request
arrives run before it, or after it with URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_TASKS=last in the
run's env.  The heap starts at 256 MiB, so that no handler is run a second
time for the heap to grow; every request is for the host 'localhost', from the
user agent 'urt', and says its time in an UrWeb-Time header.

A step can wait instead of send, for what the application does by itself:
NAME.wait holds one line, either

  wait 1min

which lets that much time pass (ms, s, min, h and d, the unit never left
out; a task periodic 10 runs six times), or

  until still running

which lets time pass until the server has printed a line holding the text
after 'until', the blanks around it aside, on stdout or stderr.  A line counts
as the recording has it: one of the server's own lines, which are dropped,
does not.  What the server did meanwhile goes under NAME, as for a request
(NAME.dml.sql, NAME.stdout, NAME.stderr); there is no response.  The clock is
moved from one thing the server is due to do to the next, so an until ends
with everything due at the time of the line and nothing after it, and the
next step comes a second later; it cannot be done if the server has nothing
more to do, or the line has not come within 365 days on the clock.  Time
passes as fast as the server does what falls due: a month of a task every
minute is some 43,000 runs of it, which take as long as they take.

A run's env adds to the application's environment, NAME=value a line, the
value taken as written, though it holds no control character but a tab;
blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped.
What urt sets itself for the above cannot be set there: URWEB_SIGFILE_PATH,
URWEB_SQLITE_DB_PATH, URWEB_DML_LOG, URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_EPOCH,
URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_PRNG_SEED, URWEB_MIN_HEAP and TZ.

A case with runs is compiled all the way, so its args cannot hold -stop or
-tc, and it fails if that does not give an application.  The compiler builds
it in urt's scratch directory, whatever the project says (-output), and from
there it is moved into the case as app.exe, where it stays, to be started by
hand if need be.
A run's args cannot hold -t: its requests are sent one after the other, and
with more threads than one it is a matter of chance which of them answers.
Nor --, after which the server would ignore the flags urt adds (the address,
the port range, -d3).  Runs need curl and sqlite3 (3.37 or later) on the PATH.

A case can also have runs in a browser, under browser/: the application
started afresh as above, once per pass, and every pass sent through it step
by step, by one user or several, each in one tab or several, each in a browser
engine -- Chromium, Firefox or WebKit.  A pass puts every user on an engine, and
there are as many as it takes to drive each through all of theirs, every user
through all three unless the run's context says fewer; the passes go at the
same time, each with its application:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- sqlite/
          `-- browser/
              `-- checkout/             a run; args, env, secret, db.sql and out/ as above
                  |-- context             the users' order, and how their browsers are set up
                  |-- files/              what the steps upload
                  |-- screenshots/        every page as drawn, with --screenshot; not for git
                  |   `-- 01-open.Alice.1.chromium.png
                  |-- recordings/         every user filmed, with --record; not for git
                  |   |-- Alice.chromium.webm
                  |   |-- Alice.chromium.vtt  a cue per line: the step and the line
                  |   `-- index.html      plays a run's films together
                  `-- interaction/
                      |-- 01-open.step      what each user does
                      |-- 01-open.timeline  what happened when: every line, every request
                      |-- 01-open.Alice.1.page.html
                      |                     the tab's page afterwards: its path, then the DOM
                      |                     as the browser has it, scripts and handlers left out
                      |-- 01-open.Alice.1.requests
                      |                     what the tab fetched, with the status of each
                      |-- 01-open.Alice.1.dialogs
                      |                     the alerts, confirms and prompts, with their answers
                      |-- 01-open.Alice.1.console
                      |                     what the tab logged, and its script's errors
                      |-- 01-open.dml.sql   what the step changed in the database, if anything
                      |-- 01-open.stdout    what the server printed meanwhile, if anything
                      |-- 01-open.stderr
                      |-- 02-add.step
                      |-- 02-add.Alice.1.page.html
                      |                     what the passes agree on, shared
                      |-- 02-add.Alice.1.page.firefox.html
                      |                     what one of them records differently, its own
                      |-- 03-pay.step
                      `-- 03-pay.error      why the step could not be done, while it cannot

A pass is named by its engine where every user is on the same one, as when
they all have the same engines: chromium, firefox, webkit.  Where they do not,
the i-th pass puts every user on the i-th of their engines in that order, the
last again for a user with fewer, and a pass that mixes them is named by the
users' engines in the context's order, joined by +: with Alice on chromium and
Bernd on firefox and webkit, chromium+firefox and chromium+webkit.
The passes agree on nearly everything, and a record they agree on is one
file; one that a pass records differently is that pass's own, beside it,
with the pass's name after the record's kind, and what that pass is compared
against.  Recording goes by the majority: the passes agreeing in the greatest
number write the shared file, the others their own, an own file whose pass
has come to agree goes, and a tie goes to the pass first in order.

A step holds lines, each for a user: 'USER: ACTION', or 'USER:' alone with the
user's lines under it, each indented with a tab.  A user is a browser of their
own, cookies and all; their tabs are pages in it, each a client to the
application, named by the action that opens them ([target=NAME]), and their
lines act on the tab in front.  The users of a step go side by side, each
through their lines in order, and the step is over when all are done and no
page has a request open but its poll; then every tab that is open is recorded,
as USER.TAB.

  Alice: open /Shop/main [target=1]
  Alice: fill 'input[name=name]' kettle
  Alice: click 'text=Add'
  Bernd:
  	open /Shop/main [target=1]
  	until 'text=1 item'

`urt-browse --help` lists the actions, their locators, which are Playwright's,
and their parameters, and what the context can say, OPTION VALUE a line, for
every user before the first line [USER] and for that user under it: the users'
order, which a run with more than one needs; whether the server's tasks due
when a request arrives run before it or after; the engines a user is driven
through ('engines chromium webkit'); a request's round trip; how
long a kind of act takes; how a hidden tab's timers are held back, as Chrome
does unless it says otherwise; and how a user's browser is set up (the window,
the colour scheme, the locale, ...), every user's being 1024x768, en-US, UTC
and the user agent 'urt' unless it says otherwise.  With --screenshot, every tab
is shot after every step as well, the whole page as the engine draws it, under
the run's screenshots/ as NAME.USER.TAB.PASS.png, the directory emptied
first: pictures to look at when a record wants explaining, which git ignores
and nothing compares, since no two engines draw alike; the records are the
same with and without.  With --record, every user is filmed on the run's
clock, under the run's recordings/ as USER.PASS.webm: their tab in front,
under a strip with their tabs, the clock and the line they are at, the pointer
and any dialog drawn in.  A frame is taken every T (--record=T, 16ms unless
given) at which anything shown may have changed, the clock stopping on those
while anything moves that will stop; a USER.PASS.vtt beside it has a cue
per line, and an index.html plays a run's films together, each in a box to
move and size at will.  The same rules as for the screenshots, and the records
the same with and without, and whatever T, too.
There is one clock, for the pages, the server and the users.  It starts at the
epoch and moves from one thing due to the next, as fast as they get done,
nothing waiting on the wall clock: an act takes the time it takes a person (a
click 400ms, a key typed 150ms), a request reaches the server half a round
trip after the page sent it and its answer the page half a round trip later
(100ms the trip), a page's timer fires when it is due, and so does a task of
the server's.  What is due at the same time is done in a fixed order: the
server's part first, then the pages' timers and the answers coming back, then
the users' input, the users in the context's order.  So a run comes out the
same every time, whatever the machine, and NAME.timeline says what happened
when, in seconds since the epoch.  What moves on its own in a page follows the
clock as well: animations, smooth scrolls, and which tab of a user's is in
front, visible and with the focus.  A last line 'wait T' in a step, for no
user, lets T pass for everyone before the records are taken.  No engine caches
or fetches a favicon, so that each sends the same requests.  A request for any
other server is refused by the driver and recorded as such: a run drives this
application, and the network is not its business; what the pages need, the
application serves (the 'file' directive).  One engine difference is left as
it is: back to a page that answered a POST sends the POST again in Chromium
and WebKit, and is refused by Firefox, where the step cannot be done and says
so; a run meant for every engine opens the page instead.
A step that cannot be done -- no such element, two of them, an until whose
element does not come in time -- stops the run, as a request that cannot be
sent does: the step's NAME.error holds the engine's complaint, beside its
records of the pages as the engine found them, the records of the steps after
it go, and the run fails until it gets past the step again.  A pass that
stops where the others go on has an own NAME.error.PASS, and own files,
empty, for what it did not record after that.
Browser runs need urt-browse beside urt, python3 with Playwright, and the
engines where PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH says; the compiler's nix-shell and its
package with urt have all of it.

Nothing else may be under snapshot/, bar a README.md beside the cases or in
one, or beside the runs or in one: a file this program would not read is
refused rather than left to rot.
The urt directory itself may hold a README.md, and a 'project' file naming
the project when the directory's name does not; other tools keep their own
directories there.


USAGE
-----

  urt snapshot [PATH...]                     update the snapshots below the given projects
  urt snapshot --whine [PATH...]             fail on a diff (you want this in CI)
  urt snapshot --screenshot [PATH...]        also shoot every page of the browser runs
  urt snapshot --record[=T] [PATH...]        also film every user of the browser runs
  urt snapshot -j[N] [PATH...]               N projects at a time, or one per core
  urt snapshot --list [PATH...]              list the cases matching the given path(s)
  urt snapshot --create [PROJECT] [CASE...]  create cases for the given project
  urt snapshot --create-run [PATH] [RUN...]  create runs for the one case at the given path
  urt snapshot --create-browser-run [PATH] [RUN...]  the same, for runs in a browser
  urt snapshot --edit [PATH] [ARTIFACT...]   edit the tracked set of a case

PATH is a project (demo/alert[.urp]), its urt directory (demo/alert.urt), its
snapshots (demo/alert.urt/snapshot), one case (demo/alert.urt/snapshot/1) or a
directory to search for snapshots.  It defaults to the current directory.

With -j N (or -jN, --jobs=N), N projects go at once, each in a process of its
own that takes the project's cases one after the other; with -j alone, as
many as there are cores.  What is printed is what a plain run prints, in the
same order: a project's lines come when its turn comes.  A browser run drives
its passes at once either way, so a project can keep three browsers busy.

With '--create', cases are set up for a project, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args' file and an 'expected' directory of empty
artifacts each.  On a terminal the artifacts to track are ticked in a
checklist and a run is offered to fill them in; otherwise all of them are
made, to be pruned by hand and filled in by a plain run.  With no PROJECT it
takes the only .urp in the current directory.

With '--create-run', runs are set up for a case, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args', a 'secret', the schema as the compiler gives it
for the case as 'db.sql' if the program has tables, and a first request to
replace.  '--create-browser-run' does the same under browser/, with a first
step, 'user: open / [target=1]', to replace.  A plain run fills in the rest.

With '--edit', the artifacts a case tracks are ticked in a checklist.  PATH
names the case, or a project or directory holding several, to choose from;
the current directory otherwise.  A newly tracked artifact starts empty, and
a run is offered to fill it in.  Named outright, the ARTIFACTs become the
tracked set without asking, for scripts.

The compiler is the one URWEB names, run with the flags in URWEB_FLAGS if
that is set, or else the in-tree build, bin/urweb under the current
directory or beside this program, run with -boot -noEmacs.  A Nix-packaged
urt has URWEB set to the compiler it was built for.

exit 0
